  <!-- ── When you need care ─────────────── -->
  <h2>Who to contact and when</h2>

  <p>
    One of the biggest adjustments is understanding the tiers of NHS care and using the right
    one. Using A&E for minor issues puts enormous strain on the system and means a very long wait
    for you.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your GP</strong> — for anything non-urgent: ongoing conditions, referrals,
      mental health, repeat prescriptions, minor illness that doesn't need same-day care
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>NHS 111</strong> — call 111 or go to <a href="https://111.nhs.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">111.nhs.uk</a>
      for urgent medical problems that aren't life-threatening. Available 24/7.
      They'll advise whether you need A&E, an urgent care centre, or can wait for a GP appointment
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Urgent Treatment Centres (UTCs)</strong> — for things that need same-day attention
      but aren't emergencies: minor injuries, infections, sprains. No referral needed.
      Find your nearest at 111.nhs.uk
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A&E (Accident & Emergency)</strong> — for genuine emergencies only: serious injuries,
      chest pain, difficulty breathing, suspected strokes. Walk in or call 999
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>999</strong> — life-threatening emergencies only. Ambulance, fire, police
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ── Prescriptions ──────────────────── -->
  <h2>Prescriptions</h2>

  <p>
    In England, there's a standard charge per prescription item — check the current amount
    at <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/nhs-services/prescriptions-and-pharmacies/nhs-prescriptions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nhs.uk</a>
    as it changes each April. Some people are exempt from charges, including: those under 16,
    full-time students aged 16–18, those over 60, pregnant women, people with certain medical
    conditions, and those receiving certain benefits.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Prepayment certificate</span>
    <p>
      If you take regular medication and need more than one prescription item per month, a
      <strong>Prescription Prepayment Certificate (PPC)</strong> lets you pay a flat rate for
      unlimited prescription items for 3 or 12 months. For many people it's significantly
      cheaper. You can buy one at <a href="https://www.nhsbsa.nhs.uk/ppc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nhsbsa.nhs.uk/ppc</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Dental ─────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Dental care</h2>

  <p>
    NHS dental care exists — but finding an NHS dentist accepting new patients can be
    genuinely difficult in London. There are three charge bands for NHS dental treatment
    (Band 1, 2 and 3), covering everything from a check-up to complex work. Current charges
    are on <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/nhs-services/dentists/dental-costs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nhs.uk</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you can't find an NHS dentist, you have two options: join waiting lists at multiple
    practices and wait, or pay for private dental care. Private dentists are easier to access
    but more expensive. Dental insurance or subscription plans (offered by most private
    practices) can help manage the cost.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Register early</span>
    <p>
      Register with a dentist as soon as you arrive — even if you don't need treatment.
      NHS dentist lists fill up fast and being on the books matters.
      Search at <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/service-search/find-a-dentist" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nhs.uk/find-a-dentist</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Mental health ──────────────────── -->
  <h2>Mental health support</h2>

  <p>
    Moving countries is a significant life event and loneliness, anxiety and overwhelm are
    genuinely common — and nothing to push through alone. The NHS has real support available,
    and you don't need to be in crisis to access it.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>NHS Talking Therapies</strong> — free, NHS-funded therapy for anxiety, depression
      and related conditions. You can <strong>self-refer</strong> — you don't need a GP referral.
      Find your local service at
      <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/talking-therapies-medicine-treatments/talking-therapies-and-counselling/nhs-talking-therapies/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nhs.uk</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Samaritans</strong> — free, confidential support, 24/7. Call 116 123.
      <a href="https://www.samaritans.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">samaritans.org</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mind</strong> — mental health charity with extensive online resources and a helpline.
      <a href="https://www.mind.org.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">mind.org.uk</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your GP</strong> — can refer you to specialist mental health services, counselling,
      and support groups if self-referral isn't available in your area
    </li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ── Private healthcare ─────────────── -->
  <h2>Private healthcare</h2>

  <p>
    Private healthcare means shorter waiting times, more choice of specialist and (usually)
    more comfortable facilities. It is not cheap. Options include:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Private health insurance</strong> — monthly premiums that cover private consultations, treatment and hospital stays. Providers include BUPA, AXA Health, Vitality and Aviva.</li>
    <li><strong>Pay as you go</strong> — many private clinics and hospitals accept self-pay without insurance, though costs add up quickly</li>
    <li><strong>Employer benefits</strong> — many London employers include private health insurance as part of a benefits package. Check your employment contract.</li>
  </ul>

